tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: The astrolabe", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Giant astrolabe: limb, almucantars, rete, star pointers, alidade.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Hellenistic Greece, perfected in the Islamic world, theory from Hipparchus (2nd century BCE); oldest surviving instruments 9th-10th century CE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A flat model of the sky: a rotating star map (rete) over a plate engraved with the horizon", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "and altitude lines for one latitude. With a sighting of one star's altitude it gives the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "time, the direction of Mecca, sunrise, and the rising sign, and it solves spherical-", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "astronomy problems by rotation.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The celestial sphere is projected stereographically onto the plane of the equator, so", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "circles stay circles. Rotating the rete until the sighted star's pointer sits on the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "measured altitude circle sets the whole sky to the current moment; the hour is read from", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "the rim.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:baghdad/house_of_wisdom", "color": "aqua"}
